# We Belong to a Lineage of Sacrifice
Tamil title: தியாகப் பரம்பரையினர் நாம்.

Just as a warrior who has drawn his sword and leapt into battle will not tremble because an elephant charges or a horse comes flying towards him, one who has made the success of a cause the purpose of life becomes impassioned and declares: “Even if I lose cattle and home, even if I lose children and kin, even if cruelties come that drink away life itself, so difficult to obtain, even if I am thrown into a pit of scorpions or rolled in a pit of fire, I shall not fear! I shall not tire!”

“We shall drive you out! We shall set an explosion for you! Look at the deadly arrows flying towards you! There, the stake stands open-mouthed to welcome you! The gallows will make the slender-waisted woman a widow!” However many such threats may come, a person who has dedicated himself to a noble cause acquires the disposition to regard them all as dust.

Had that disposition never arisen, Greece would not have produced a Socrates. Germany would not have seen a Martin Luther. Bohemia would not have produced the great reformer Jan Hus. Savonarola would not have hung upon the gallows. Latimer would not have looked towards Ridley, ready to die, and said courageously, “Brother, today we offer ourselves because we entered the work of religious reform. They are going to bind us and burn us. The fire we light will not easily be extinguished,” before advancing to die in the flames.

The world still cannot forget those activists who possessed the conviction that their principles would benefit humankind and redeem the society in which they were born and raised, and who therefore “demonstrated through death” that they would surrender even life for those principles. Many years have passed, yet they remain. The noble Jesus and Muhammad, who was stoned for introducing a new doctrine, remain in memory because of the hardships and losses they endured for their convictions.

Siddhartha was born in a golden palace, with a tender-bodied woman who rivalled gold upon a silken bed, attendants to play the veena and massage his soft feet, a place without hardship into which not even an atom of distress could enter. Why, even after 2,500 years, is he praised in every corner of the world as incomparable, exalted and noble? He dedicated himself to the public good. He surrendered status and every comfort!

The names of sacred people who thus gave life, possessions and everything else to the public good will endure for ever, though the flood of time runs on and storms of destruction strike.

It is because they know this truth that the lions of our rationalist movement labour without fear or fatigue, whether struck by stones or words, threatened with a knife, abused as villain and scoundrel, warned by a voice crying “Beware!”, or bullied with “You insignificant ant! Just see what happens to you!” We know clearly the histories of such threatening voices falling silent, and of tsars, Louis, Charles and guardians who plotted repeatedly only to lose their own position. From the beginning, therefore, such bullying and threats have tasted to us like red honey and ripe plantain.

“He is an atheist! He says everything that must not be said. He doubts everything. He asks why and how. He wishes to separate. He demands a Dravidian country!” The rulers of domination said all manner of things like this and tried as far as possible to suppress us. They failed not merely because we were ready to sacrifice for our principles.

They failed because of the firmness and strength within the principles themselves!

Sacred pioneers of new ideas have been scorched, but their doctrines have never thereby been reduced to ashes. The Buddha left his lofty palace to serve the people; his teachings did not therefore end upon the rubbish heap. The sacrifice shown by each of them gave their principles greater vigour and strength.

Who is Nedunchezhian? Could he not have passed his time with his college education and account books? Why do countless young people like him travel from town to town, forgetting even their bodies and leaving their own homes, regarding labour for the community as a great blessing? Why must all of them come forward in this way? In a world filled with orchards of fruit trees, groves of palms, flying birds, leaping deer, chattering parrots, clusters of grapes, plantains, mangoes, fields and waterfalls, why should they not simply attend to their own occupations? Why must they undertake this work that invites gunfire, cry “Principle! Principle!” and offer themselves either to bullets or diseases of the internal organs? If we consider why this is necessary, there come to mind the noble people who reformed the world and the list of heroes who surrendered their lives to give life to their nations.

Our own people stand in that line today. This is truth, not empty praise. They stand in the list of incomparable thinkers who surrendered themselves to the public good and endured everything: being cast into fire or tied in a cattle shed, threatened with shooting or poison, confronted with deceit, and called dogs and demons. They walk the road of countless liberation heroes such as Rousseau, Mazzini, Garibaldi and Marx.

The intellectual lineage is immense! Many heirs of that great family live in many places in this century, and we of the DMK are among them!

“Is it religious domination? We shall not yield. Is it domination by money? We have no thought of submission. Is it a life of slavery? We shall never tolerate it!” We make this known every day.

We do not possess the cunning that turns the people's minds into a volcano and then immediately into a block of ice, bending the road into twists and obscurities. We read the histories of the intellectual lineage. The hardships and losses they suffered continue to cultivate the quality of sacrifice in our hearts. Uttering their sacred names, we labour to remove evil.

There were people who refused to accept that we laboured in this fashion. Many crowed, “What arrogance these fellows display!” Some asked, “Can a sparrow become a hawk? Can these little fellows become warriors of liberation?” We have now grown to such an extent that all of them must cover their faces, while those who dreamed of destroying us through blustering speech have run and hidden. The people's assembly continues to support us.

This is what the Tiruchirappalli conference has demonstrated to the world.

This is the lesson taught by the beauty, distinction, radiance and strength of the conference!

We are not insignificant people of no account; we are a lineage of sacrifice! The principles we proclaim are not things so timid that they turn cold at a touch. They make the people's welfare their highest concern. Our warriors who labour to spread them regard comforts as trivial, like the Buddha. Like Socrates, they possess the courage to drink poison and die if it is offered. Fire! The boom of an explosion! They have dedicated themselves against everything. They are Mazzinis and Marxes who do not fear repression!

Our people unquestionably possess the firmness of mind to endure even if all the cruelties borne by the noble reformers of the world were gathered together and inflicted upon them. That is why we say that we too have become part of the intellectual lineage.

This sacrificial disposition and selfless character are the wealth we possess today. It is because the poor have recognised them that we are able to build and safeguard so great a movement.

The government threatens us. It is this courage that makes us regard the threat as trivial.

Though the rulers of domination attempt to kill us through every kind of stratagem, it is our confidence in the principles that stirs us to say, “Stand fast, enemy! The spear comes leaping!”

It is because the people's assembly has seen this confidence and the hardships we bear through it that it blesses us. The DMK grows day by day.

Is this growth enough? We say it is never enough!

We who seek to establish a separate country still have lakhs of hearts to enlighten and hundreds of villages and hamlets to visit. We are not merely reformers; we must make a new homeland blossom! Is this an ordinary responsibility? No! No!

Therefore, even as we rejoice in the success of the conference, we must think about our future. We are messengers of a magnificent intellectual lineage. We know the sacrifices that lineage accepted in order to realise its ideals!

> Long have you rolled upon a silken bed;
> now try lying upon the hard ground.
> Learn to roll upon burning sand
> and immerse yourself in cold water.
> How long will you wander singing like a bulbul?
> How long will you live in a cool grove?
> Build a nest upon the mountain summit;
> train yourself!

Calling to mind these lines by the poet of freedom, Iqbal, we must do still more to draw the countless brothers who live without an ideal towards the lineage of sacrifice.

Sacrifice and labour are never wasted!

Ours is a great family!

Let us unite its elder and younger brothers.

Let us labour by establishing more branches of the movement than before in every town and by conducting campaigns.

Truth will always triumph.

A new homeland will surely blossom in the red blood of sacrifice.

(27-2-26, *Dravida Nadu*)

---NOTES---
The source's `பொ?மியா` is the OCR-damaged country name “Bohemia,” associated here with Jan Hus. The quoted Latimer-Ridley wording is translated from Anna's Tamil retelling rather than substituted with the familiar English historical quotation. The damaged slogan `தார நில்பகையே!துள்ளிவருகுது வேல்` is rendered conservatively as “Stand fast, enemy! The spear comes leaping.” The closing phrase `தியாகச் செந்நரில்` appears to mean “in the red blood of sacrifice.” The publication year `26` is contextually suspect and requires scan verification; it remains unchanged in the verbatim source and date line.
